 region is roughly proportional to p (and p \u2212 1 forward warmup slots); its *relative* cost scales as ~p/M (1F1B) so small M causes severe bubble overhead. ZB-1p/ZB-2p reduce the warmup requirement (the paper derives warmup bounds smaller than p \u2212 1 because W-passes can be deferred), so ZB-2p should suffer least at small M. Testing M \u2264 p, M = p, and M > p directly probes the regime where the paper predicts the ZB-2p advantage.\n- **Two pipeline depths (p = 4, 8)**: the paper's analysis shows bubble overhead grows with p, so the low-M penalty should be larger at p = 8; the design tests whether ZB-2p's advantage strengthens with depth, and provides a robustness check that H1/H2 are not artifacts of one depth.\n- **M grid {2, 4, 8, 16, 32}**: powers of two spanning well below and above both depths, including the transition region around M = p where 1F1B's warmup (p \u2212 1) first fits inside a steady-state cycle.\n- **3 repetitions**: pipeline timing noise (kernel launch jitter, clock variability) is modest but non-zero; 3 reps allow a simple mean \u00b1 spread (and a sign/pairwise test) without inflating runtime.\n\n## (c) Explicit controls\n\n1. **Identical model** across all cells (same architecture, parameter count, layer sequence, dtype).\n2. **Identical microbatch size** across all cells (per (a), this is the held-fixed workload unit).\n3. **Identical pipeline stage partition** across schedules at a given depth (fixed, even split; no per-schedule re-tuning \u2014 this is what \"fixed-split mode\" means here). *Assumption*: the reference implementation exposes a flag to disable the search/split-search mode; if not, fixed splits are imposed by construction.\n4. **Fixed interleaving factor for 1F1B-I**: model chunks per stage fixed at the same value in every 1F1B-I cell (e.g., 2 chunks per stage, matching the paper's typical interleaved configuration). *Assumption*: exact default chunk count; confirm against implementation when available.\n5. **Identical data ordering and seeds**: per repetition r \u2208 {1,2,3}, seed_r identical across schedules; input data synthetic or a fixed dataset with fixed ordering, since throughput measurement is insensitive to data content but sensitive to input sizes.\n6. **Warmup/drain handling**: primary metric is **steady-state throughput** \u2014 measure over the central iteration window after a fixed warmup of N_warm iterations and stop before drain, or equivalently time a fixed number of full pipeline passes and exclude the first N_warm and last N_drain passes from the timed window. N_warm chosen \u2265 2p iterations so all schedules reach steady state. Report the warmup/drain exclusion rule identically for all schedules. *Assumption*: iteration-level timing instrumentation is available; if only end-to-end wall-clock is available, use a long iteration count (e.g., 64+ iterations per run) so warmup/drain are amortized and report the method as an approximation.\n7. **Same optimizer/loss configuration** (irrelevant to timing in steady state but keeps runs identical); gradients not synced across pipeline replicas in a way that differs by schedule.\n8. **Hardware hygiene** (for the future run): lock clocks where permitted, one process per device, no co-located jobs; report GPU model, driver, interconnect (NVLink/PCIe) as covariates.\n\n## (d) Analysis plan\n\n1. **Primary data table**: for each (schedule, p, M, rep): steady-state samples/sec; secondary: bubble fraction if per-stage timeline/timestamp instrumentation exists (e.g., fraction of device time idle between forward/backward ops). *Assumption*: bubble fraction requires per-stage timeline tracing; if unavailable, it is reported as \"not measurable\" rather than approximated.\n2. **Throughput-vs-M curves**: per schedule and per depth, plot mean samples/sec vs. M with per-rep points shown. Expectations: 1F1B/1F1B-I curves rise steeply from M = 2 toward an asymptote near M \u2248 p and flatten for M > p; ZB-1p rises with a smaller low-M penalty; ZB-2p is near-flat across M.\n3. **Monotonicity test (H1)**: for each of 1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p at each depth, test whether mean throughput is strictly increasing in M across the observed range. Formal test: a monotone trend test across M levels (e.g., Page's trend test across reps, or a simple sign test on successive differences M=2\u21924\u21928\u219216\u219232), plus a descriptive effect-size check (percent gain from M = p to M = p/2, and from M = p to M = 2p). Decision rule: H1 supported if throughput is monotonically non-decreasing with a significant positive low-M gain (e.g., \u2265 5% from M < p to M = p region) for all three schedules at both depths.\n4. **ZB-2p low-M robustness (H2)**: define ZB-2p's high-M plateau as the mean throughput at the largest M (32); compute low-M retention ratio R = throughput(M = 2)/throughput(M = 32) per depth. Compare R(ZB-2p) against R(1F1B), R(1F1B-I), R(ZB-1p) via the rep-level spread (overlap of \u00b11 s.d. or a pairwise rank test, n = 3 per cell). H2 supported if ZB-2p's retention ratio exceeds each baseline's by a clear margin (e.g., \u2265 10 percentage points) at both depths.\n5. **Depth interaction**: check whether the ZB-2p low-M advantage grows from p = 4 to p = 8 (predicted by the ~p/M bubble model).\n6. **Robustness/sensitivity**: verify conclusions hold under (i) the optional search-mode extension if run, (ii) reporting both raw samples/sec and throughput normalized to each schedule's own maximum-M value (removes any constant per-schedule overhead so the shape-vs-M comparison is fair).\n\n## (e) Feasibility statement (estimates only \u2014 clearly not measured)\n\n- **Hardware requirement**: p-way pipeline parallelism needs p device groups; the design needs runs at p = 4 and p = 8, so **8 accelerators minimum** (a single 8-GPU node suffices; 1F1B-I interleaving additionally requires enough memory per device for 2 model chunks). *Assumption*: an 8-GPU node (e.g., A100/H100-class) of the kind used in the referenced paper's benchmarks.\n- **Runtime estimate**: with a modest model (~100M\u20131B parameters) and ~64 timed iterations plus warmup per run, each timed run is minutes-scale (order 1\u20135 min including setup). 120 runs \u2192 roughly **4\u201310 GPU-hours** for the main grid; the optional search extension adds search overhead per (p, M) cell, plausibly doubling total time. These are order-of-magnitude estimates based on typical pipeline-parallel microbenchmark costs, not measurements.\n- **Software feasibility**: requires an implementation exposing all four schedules with a common runner; the referenced paper's codebase is the natural candidate but is absent from this environment, so availability is an assumption. No GPU exists in the current sandbox; execution is out of scope here by the task brief.\n- **Cost/feasibility caveats**: M = 32 at p = 8 with interleaving has the highest memory pressure (2 model chunks \u00d7 deep pipeline); if OOM, reduce model size rather than microbatch size (microbatch size must stay fixed per controls).\n\n## Summary of assumptions (vs. fixed requirements)\n- *Fixed by task/researcher*: design-only; schedules named; low-M regime = M \u2264 p with test points M < p, M = p, M > p; primary metric = steady-state samples/sec, bubble fraction secondary; fixed-split primary, search optional; grid schedules \u00d7 M \u2208 {2,4,8,16,32} \u00d7 p \u2208 {4,8} \u00d7 \u22653 reps.\n- *Assumptions (not verifiable in this environment)*: the four schedules and their warmup/bubble properties as published (Qi et al., 2024); existence of an implementation with schedule flags, fixed-split mode, and iteration-level timing; interleaving factor value for 1F1B-I; microbatch size fixed with global batch scaling in M as the sweep convention; 8-GPU node availability and minutes-per-run runtime; feasibility of bubble-fraction instrumentation.\n\nNo experiment has been run; all expected outcomes above are predictions to be tested, not results.",